    "lawCode": "Amd §745, rpld §747-a, RPAP L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Actions and Proceedings Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4526\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the real property actions and\nproceedings law, in relation to summary proceedings to recover\npossession of real property; and to repeal certain provisions of such\nlaw relating to judgments in non-payment proceedings in the city of New\nYork\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis legislation would remove a provision of the 1997 Rent Regulation\nReform Act that required judges to order tenants to deposit outstanding\nrent during the course of an eviction case, thereby allowing tenants a\nmore fair and equitable process during eviction proceedings.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends subdivision 2 of section 745 of the real property\nactions and proceeding law.\n\nSection 2 repeals section 747-a of the real property actions and\nproceedings law.\n\nSection 3: sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe 1997 Rent Regulation Reform Act amended Section 745 of the RPAPL to\nrequire judges to order tenants to deposit outstanding rent during the\ncourse of an eviction case, and mandated the dismissal of the tenants'\ndefenses if they could not afford the deposit. As a result, thousands of\nlow income tenants were prevented from asserting bona fide defenses,\nincluding rent overcharge and breach of the warranty of habitability,\nbecause they were financially unable to deposit an amount of arrears\nthat ultimately might have proven illegal.  Such tenants were deprived\nof the basic due process right to be heard on their legal claims and\ndefenses, simply because they were poor.  Many were evicted as a result,\nin derogation of the purposes of New York's tenant protection laws. This\nlegislation aims to end this injustice and insure that all tenants, both\nhigh and low income, have the right to litigate their claims and\ndefenses in eviction proceedings. The legislation further aims to ensure\nthat low income tenants are not compelled to undergo trials before they\nhave an opportunity to access the legal help available under NYC's\nUniversal Access to Counsel program.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
